{"causes":["To a degree of independence from the Carolingian Empire","The need for resources and trade with other civilizations","The influence of Christianity in Scandinavia"],"effects":["The spread of Christianity throughout Scandinavia","The establishment of trade networks across Europe","The development of medieval city economies"],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"carolingian-empire","sourceName":"Carolingian Empire","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"vikings","targetName":"Vikings","context":"The Vikings' expansion was driven by the need for resources and trade with other civilizations influenced by the Carolingian Empire."},{"sourceSlug":"byzantine-empire","sourceName":"Byzantine Empire","verb":"TRANSFORMS","targetSlug":"vikings","targetName":"Vikings","context":"The Vikings engaged in significant cultural exchange with the Byzantine Empire, adopting new technologies and artistic styles."},{"sourceSlug":"islamic-world","sourceName":"Islamic World","verb":"TRANSFORMS","targetSlug":"vikings","targetName":"Vikings","context":"The Vikings traded with the Islamic world, introducing new goods and ideas to Scandinavia."}],"places":[{"name":"Scandinavia","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Hamburg","role":"Major settlement"},{"name":"London","role":"Major settlement"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Vikings","type":"Reference article","slug":"vikings"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q12567","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vikings"],"tags":["culture","Economic Theories & Systems","economic-theory"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Vikings is remembered as a landmark idea of the Classical era, referenced in 155 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Influenced intellectual traditions and cultural development.","summary":"The Vikings were the Norse seafaring peoples of Scandinavia (modern Denmark, Norway, and Sweden) who, during the Viking Age (c. 793–1066 CE), undertook an extraordinary period of exploration, raiding, trading, colonisation, and political expansion that brought them into contact with — and frequently transformed — civilisations from Newfoundland in the west to Constantinople and Baghdad in the east. The term 'Viking' (Old Norse víkingr) originally referred specifically to raiders and pirates, though in modern usage it has come to describe the entirety of the Norse culture of the period. The Vikings were not a unified people or polity but rather a diverse set of communities sharing language, material culture, and religious traditions (Old Norse religion, later gradually replaced by Christianity), who operated across an enormous geographic range.

The Viking Age is conventionally dated from the raid on the monastery of Lindisfarne (793 CE) — the first major recorded Norse raid on Britain — through the Norman Conquest of England by William the Conqueror (1066 CE), a Norman whose lineage traced back to the Viking Rollo who had been granted Normandy by the French king in 911 CE. During this period, Norse peoples settled Iceland (c. 874 CE), Greenland (c. 985 CE), and briefly Vinland (L'Anse aux Meadows, Newfoundland, c. 1000 CE) — the first European settlements in the Americas. In the east, the Varangians (Norse traders and warriors who penetrated Russia via the river systems) founded the Kievan Rus' state, served as the elite Varangian Guard of the Byzantine emperors, and reached Constantinople and Baghdad. In England, the Danelaw established Norse political control over northern and eastern England. In France, Normans founded a duchy that would eventually conquer England and establish a kingdom in southern Italy.

The Vikings' legacy is deeply ambivalent: they were raiders and slavers who terrorised coastal and riverside communities across Europe, but also skilled craftsmen, traders who connected the Baltic to the Mediterranean and the Islamic world, pioneering navigators who mastered open-ocean sailing by stars and the sun, and settlers who populated the North Atlantic islands. Norse mythology and literature — the Eddas, the sagas — represent a sophisticated literary and religious tradition.","subjects":["Viking Age","Medieval History","Classical Era","Scandinavia","Medieval Europe","Exploration","Norse Culture","Migration"],"frameworks":["WORLD_SYSTEMS","STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Vikings' impact on European history was significant, shaping the course of politics, culture, and trade networks. Their exploration of new lands and trade networks laid the groundwork for the medieval economy and the rise of cities such as Hamburg and London.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
